---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Harness Engineering"
  description="Eric Nolan's evidence-driven work on the systems surrounding AI models: context, memory, tools, governance, orchestration, verification, and bounded automation."
>
  <header class="subhero subhero--harness">
    <div class="shell subhero-grid">
      <div class="subhero-copy">
        <div class="subhero-badges">
          <span>AI SYSTEMS</span>
          <span>RUNTIME DESIGN</span>
          <span>GOVERNANCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="subhero-kicker">HARNESS ENGINEERING // SYSTEMS AROUND THE MODEL</p>
        <h1>The model is not the product.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Context, memory, instructions, tools, routing, state, evaluation, approval, and execution boundaries determine what an AI system can know, do, and recover from.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-pop" href="https://github.com/unyieldingclaw-dev/harness-engineering">Open the research repo →</a>
          <a class="button secondary case-secondary" href="#system-map">Map the harness ↓</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="harness-map" id="system-map" aria-label="Harness Engineering system map">
        <div class="harness-core">MODEL</div>
        <div class="harness-node node-context"><span>CONTEXT</span><small>authority · freshness</small></div>
        <div class="harness-node node-memory"><span>MEMORY</span><small>state · provenance</small></div>
        <div class="harness-node node-tools"><span>TOOLS</span><small>contracts · permissions</small></div>
        <div class="harness-node node-orchestration"><span>ORCHESTRATION</span><small>routing · handoffs</small></div>
        <div class="harness-node node-governance"><span>GOVERNANCE</span><small>scope · approval</small></div>
        <div class="harness-node node-evaluation"><span>EVALUATION</span><small>evidence · behavior</small></div>
        <div class="harness-node node-runtime"><span>RUNTIME</span><small>retries · recovery</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section harness-thesis">
    <div class="shell thesis-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The thesis</p>
        <h2>Prompt quality is not enough.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="thesis-copy">
        <p>
          AI products quickly accumulate prompts, rules, memory, tools, routers, evaluators, retries, approval gates, and automation. Those pieces decide whether the system is merely impressive in a demo or dependable enough for real work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Harness Engineering is my attempt to treat that surrounding layer as engineering rather than as a bag of prompting tricks.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark harness-principles">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading section-heading--punch">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Current research themes</p>
          <h2>Where the hard questions actually live.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">The framework keeps circling the same engineering tensions.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="harness-question-grid">
        <article class="harness-question">
          <span>01 // CONTEXT</span>
          <h3>Which source wins?</h3>
          <p>When instructions, project memory, runtime evidence, and user intent disagree, authority needs an explicit order.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="harness-question">
          <span>02 // EXECUTION</span>
          <h3>What may the model do?</h3>
          <p>Automation should have bounded authority, deterministic stops, and approval gates where consequences deserve judgment.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="harness-question">
          <span>03 // VERIFICATION</span>
          <h3>Who checks the checker?</h3>
          <p>Independent evidence matters. The same reasoning path should not be the only authority judging its own output.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="harness-question">
          <span>04 // OBSERVABILITY</span>
          <h3>What evidence already exists?</h3>
          <p>Do not install telemetry because the diagram looks incomplete. Measure the real gap before adding machinery.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section harness-rule">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="rule-card">
        <span class="rule-card__index">RULE 001</span>
        <blockquote>No new machinery without an observed need.</blockquote>
        <p>
          Use deterministic sources where they already exist. Measure first. Add agents, telemetry, or infrastructure only when an observed failure mode earns the complexity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
